


SLEEP(3)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual		 SLEEP(3)



NNAAMMEE
     sslleeeepp, usleep - suspend process execution

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<uunniissttdd..hh>>

     unsigned int
     sleep(seconds)
	   unsigned int seconds;

     void
     usleep(microseconds)
	    long microseconds;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sslleeeepp function suspends execution of the calling process
     for _s_e_c_o_n_d_s of clock time, or until interrupted by a signal.

     The uusslleeeepp function suspends execution of the calling pro-
     cess for _m_i_c_r_o_s_e_c_o_n_d_s of clock time, or until interrupted by
     a signal.

     System activity may lengthen the suspension.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The sslleeeepp function returns 0, or if interrupted before
     _s_e_c_o_n_d_s, the amount not slept (the requested time minus the
     time actually slept) in seconds.  The uusslleeeepp function does
     not return anything (meaningful).

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     select(2)

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     Previous implementations of sslleeeepp and uusslleeeepp re-suspended
     the process if interrupted by a signal.  This implementation
     has been changed to return in that case, to conform to POSIX
     1003.1-88.

     On the PDP-11 the previous version of uusslleeeepp took a _u__i_n_t as
     the input parameter.  This has been changed to be _l_o_n_g so
     that uusslleeeepp can be used for more than 65 milliseconds (a
     u_int could only count 65535 microseconds) of sleep.  Thus
     it is now possible for uusslleeeepp to handle longer sleep dura-
     tions than sslleeeepp.

BBUUGGSS
     On the PDP-11 the clock resolution is limited to the line
     frequency (usually 60Hz in the U.S.A. and 50Hz elsewhere).

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A uusslleeeepp function appeared in 4.3BSD.  A sslleeeepp function



